:: WatchSticksAndStones [childhood]
# Games of Strategy

You watch as a small group of children play a game with stones and sticks. They've created a pattern of small holes in the dirt, and they move stones between these holes according to specific rules.

The children's faces are serious with concentration as they consider each move. Sometimes they debate the rules or explain strategies to younger players.

An older boy named Agnis notices your interest. "This game teaches us to think ahead," he explains. "Just like when planning a hunt or deciding where to find food tomorrow."

You observe how the players must anticipate their opponent's moves and plan their own strategy several steps in advance. When someone makes a particularly clever move, the others nod in appreciation.

Though you don't yet understand all the rules, you begin to see patterns in the game. It seems to involve counting, planning, and understanding consequences - all skills that will be useful throughout your life.

